what upbeat note there  are some planets where we do actually  know everything that we need to start  asking the really interesting physics  question  so there are those planets which both  transit and have had radial velocity  data taken and from that you can get  both the mass and the radius and that's  incredibly important because then you  get density and density is actually when  you can start comparing two things that  we understand here on earth so here's a  little plot of some of the better-known  better characterize small planets so you  can see Earth and Venus down there in  the corner and then the lines are lines  of constant density and the bottom red  line would be a world made up entirely  of iron so that's basically the most  dense thing that we can imagine  realistically forming around another  star and you can see nothing is  particularly close to that line and then  the top blue line is is majority water  with some other minerals and you can see  there are quite a few things up but when  the interesting things here is  particularly when you look at the  position of Earth and Venus which are  planets that we hopefully understand  relatively well you can see there's a  huge range there's this world called  Kepler 11f way up there at the top there  much much less dense than any of the  small worlds in our own solar system and  so now we can start finding out you know  what's you know trying to ask the  questions why why is that how way to  that form why is it so different than  the worlds that we have here and then  from those gold standards there's even a  better set the brightest ones that are  nearest to earth we can actually do a  method of detecting what's in their  atmospheres now not all of them have  atmospheres but a few of them do and  we've gone to the point we've detected  molecules in the atmospheres around  planets around other stars this is just  beginning and as a area very active  research in astronomy but we've we found  some of the basic molecules water carbon  oxide carbon dioxide methane and we're  building up a library again we haven't  found anything that looks like any of  the planets in our own solar system and  so it's opening up even more questions  of why is that you know why is our solar  system like it is in fact and so this is  an area where we're it's there's going  to be a lot of new to  govers in the next several years so I  know I'm standing between you and lunch  so let me just sum up by saying that you  know in the last 25 years we've  discovered over 1,700 planets around  other stars but the process of really  understanding them is only just  beginning because we've only just gotten  to the point where we have enough data  to actually start challenging physical  hypotheses against this data and so what  that requires not only preserving the  data which is really not that difficult  but actually understanding its concerts  context and the biases which is turning  out to be a much more difficult problem  I will end in an optimistic note in the  exoplanets are very big right now and  there are many many missions both in the  planning stages and underway that are  going to expand are these counts  tremendously over the next three years  these are just three that I'm  highlighting because they're large space  missions but once they're done we're  going to have tens of thousands of  exoplanets that we know thank you
 

